WEBVTT

NOTE
Captions for the voiceover only. The film's own on-screen lines are burned in and
are deliberately not repeated here. Cue boundaries come from the speech onsets in
the voice stem of the mix, not from the storyboard, so they track the read.

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:02.900
Your agent says it's done.

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.200
So you check, you prompt again…

3
00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:06.250
…and again.

4
00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:11.850
runcastle closes the gap
between done, and actually done.

5
00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:13.350
It starts small.

6
00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:16.700
Something looks off, so you grab it,
and keep moving.

7
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.500
When you're ready, you talk it through,

8
00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:22.500
and the agent turns the conversation
into a plan.

9
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:25.600
Small tickets, in order.

10
00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:27.750
Then you hit burn.

11
00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:31.600
Each one gets its own agent,
sealed in a container,

12
00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:33.450
all building at once.

13
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.900
And before anyone says done,

14
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.200
an agent opens the app,
and actually uses it.

15
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.600
So you're not taking its word for it.

16
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.500
You watch the recording,
and point at what's wrong.

17
00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:50.350
And when main has moved on without you,

18
00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:52.000
it sorts that out too.

19
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.750
This time, it's actually done.

20
00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.850
That's runcastle,
the IDE for coding agents.
